/// \defgroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest Special power and upgrade module factories, the rest
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief The remaining special-power and upgrade module factories that
///        \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories,
///        \ref grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more and
///        \ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories do not cover, and the
///        module classes they create.
///
/// Every function in this group is one half of a registration made by
/// ModuleFactory::init: an instance factory (\c friend_newModuleInstance),
/// which builds a module for one object, or a data factory
/// (\c friend_newModuleData), which builds the shared module data from an
/// INI block. \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories describes the
/// flow from a module line in an \c Object block to a module step by step;
/// it applies here unchanged. This page covers what differs for the
/// classes listed below.
///
/// \par What is documented here
/// - Instance factories of ten module classes: ActivateModuleSpecialPower,
///   AudioLoopUpgrade, BaseUpgrade, CurseSpecialPower,
///   EvacuateGarrisonSpecialPower, HordeDispatchSpecialPower,
///   SpecialPowerModule, TeleportToCasterSpecialPower,
///   UnleashSpecialPower and WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule.
/// - Data factories of fifteen module data classes: those of
///   CashHackSpecialPower, CombineHordeSpecialPower, CurseSpecialPower,
///   StoreObjectsSpecialPower, TeleportToCasterSpecialPower and
///   WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule, and those of the upgrade modules
///   ArmorUpgrade, AudioLoopUpgrade, BaseUpgrade, CommandPointsUpgrade,
///   CostModifierUpgrade, ModelConditionUpgrade, RemoveUpgradeUpgrade,
///   ReplaceSelfUpgrade and UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade.
/// - CombineHordeSpecialPower, StoreObjectsSpecialPower,
///   CommandPointsUpgrade, RemoveUpgradeUpgrade and ReplaceSelfUpgrade
///   already have class pages in the sibling groups; the data factories
///   here link to them. The other factories of the classes below (for
///   example the instance factories of CashHackSpecialPower, ArmorUpgrade
///   and CostModifierUpgrade) are not described here.
///
/// \par Registrations
/// - Every name is spelled exactly like its module class, and every one is
///   registered as a logic (behavior) module, so an \c Object block lists
///   it on a `Behavior = <name> ModuleTag_...` line
///   (\ref page_modding_modules). The registrations are listed in
///   `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`.
/// - One registration breaks the usual pattern of a module class plus a
///   data class named after it: the name \c WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule
///   pairs WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule's instance factory with the data
///   factory of WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModuleData.
/// - Look up a factory by its registration, not by the name of the source
///   file under \c Code/ that holds it.
/// - Read with Zero Hour's bit values (`Common/Module.h`), the interface
///   masks sort the classes into three kinds:
///   - **Special power only**: \c SpecialPowerModule,
///     CashHackSpecialPower, CombineHordeSpecialPower,
///     HordeDispatchSpecialPower and \c WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule.
///   - **Update only**: ActivateModuleSpecialPower, CurseSpecialPower,
///     EvacuateGarrisonSpecialPower, StoreObjectsSpecialPower,
///     TeleportToCasterSpecialPower and UnleashSpecialPower. Despite their
///     names these are special-ability update modules: their data starts
///     from SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///     ActivateModuleSpecialPower, CurseSpecialPower,
///     TeleportToCasterSpecialPower and UnleashSpecialPower act in a
///     \c triggerAbilityEffect override after
///     SpecialAbilityUpdate::triggerAbilityEffect;
///     EvacuateGarrisonSpecialPower acts in a \c finishAbility override.
///   - **Upgrade modules**: every upgrade name here includes the upgrade
///     bit. AudioLoopUpgrade's mask is update, die and upgrade, which its
///     code fits (it has an update step, a \c KillOnDeath check and an
///     upgrade step). The other upgrade masks add bits this page does not
///     interpret, as on \ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - All ten instance factories have one shape: allocate the class's size
///   with the global \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory), run the class's
///   constructor with the object and the module data inside an exception
///   frame that frees the block if the constructor throws, and return the
///   result as a Module pointer. When the allocation returns null, the
///   constructor is skipped and null is returned. The bodies differ only
///   in the size and the constructor; a size too large for a one-byte
///   operand makes the body three bytes longer.
/// - Fourteen of the fifteen data factories share one shape as well:
///   allocate, run the data class's default constructor inside an
///   exception frame, then, when the INI pointer is not null, call
///   INI::initFromINIMultiProc with the new data and a field-parse
///   procedure. ArmorUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData is the
///   exception: it has no exception frame, so nothing frees the block if
///   the constructor throws (the shorter form
///   \ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories describes).
/// - In every data factory here the parse step does not test the
///   allocation: a null block with a non-null INI pointer would still be
///   handed to the parser. Parsing happens after the exception frame has
///   closed, so an INI error thrown while parsing does not free the new
///   data. Zero Hour's data factory macro has the same order.
/// - Allocation sizes say something about per-object state.
///   CashHackSpecialPower, CombineHordeSpecialPower,
///   HordeDispatchSpecialPower and WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule are
///   allocated at SpecialPowerModule's size, so they add no per-object
///   fields of their own. ActivateModuleSpecialPower, CurseSpecialPower,
///   EvacuateGarrisonSpecialPower, TeleportToCasterSpecialPower and
///   UnleashSpecialPower share one size; StoreObjectsSpecialPower is
///   larger. BaseUpgrade, ArmorUpgrade, ModelConditionUpgrade and
///   UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade share one size; AudioLoopUpgrade and
///   CostModifierUpgrade are larger.
/// - Zero Hour generates both factories inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory
///   pool and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the
///   global \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par Field tables
/// Each class page lists the fields its data class accepts, read from the
/// retail executable's own field tables, which the
/// \c buildFieldParse procedures pass to MultiIniFieldParse::add. The
/// procedures add a shared table first and then the class's own:
/// - the special-power table (SpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse),
///   listed in full on \ref grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more;
/// - the special-ability table
///   (SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse), which includes
///   \c SpecialPowerTemplate, \c StartAbilityRange, \c AbilityAbortRange,
///   \c PreparationTime, \c PackTime, \c UnpackTime and many more;
/// - the shared upgrade table, with \c TriggeredBy, \c ConflictsWith,
///   \c RequiresAllTriggers, \c RequiresAllConflictingTriggers,
///   \c CustomAnimAndDuration and \c Permanent
///   (\ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories explains them).
///
/// \par Origins
/// SpecialPowerModule, CashHackSpecialPower, ArmorUpgrade,
/// CostModifierUpgrade, ModelConditionUpgrade and UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade
/// exist in Zero Hour. AudioLoopUpgrade, BaseUpgrade,
/// CombineHordeSpecialPower and WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule are not in
/// Zero Hour but appear in Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree.
/// ActivateModuleSpecialPower, CurseSpecialPower,
/// EvacuateGarrisonSpecialPower, HordeDispatchSpecialPower,
/// StoreObjectsSpecialPower, TeleportToCasterSpecialPower and
/// UnleashSpecialPower appear in neither, so for them the BFME 2 bodies
/// are the only source.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their tags, and the
///   fields on the class pages. The special power a module answers to is a
///   separate \c SpecialPower definition named by its
///   \c SpecialPowerTemplate field; upgrades are separate \c Upgrade
///   definitions named in \c TriggeredBy and \c ConflictsWith
///   (\ref page_core_frameworks_upgrades).
/// - Field names must be spelled as the retail tables spell them,
///   including \c ObeyRechageOnTrigger on UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade.
/// - Hard-coded: the module names, which data factory each name uses, and
///   what each class does with its fields. A new kind of effect needs a
///   new class and a new ModuleFactory::init registration.
/// - All of these run in the game logic, so the multiplayer and save rules
///   on \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories apply: every machine
///   must load the same values, and an object saves each module's state
///   under its module tag.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Time fields here include the kinds below, and a change to the logic
/// rate (\ref page_frame_loop) affects them differently. The list is
/// partial:
/// - \c KillAfterMS (AudioLoopUpgrade) and the special-ability table's
///   millisecond fields, such as \c PreparationTime, \c PackTime,
///   \c UnpackTime and \c EffectDuration, go through
///   INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt, which turns milliseconds into whole
///   logic frames once, when the INI is read
///   (\ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories has the details).
/// - \c ToggleOnSleepFrames and \c ToggleOffSleepFrames
///   (WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule) are read as plain integers and used
///   directly as logic-frame counts, so their real-time length scales with
///   the logic rate.
/// - \c TempConditionTime (ModelConditionUpgrade) and the special-power
///   table's \c SetModelConditionTime are read as plain reals; the code
///   that consumes them is not described here, so their unit is not
///   established.
/// - \c CustomAnimAndDuration (shared upgrade table and special-ability
///   table) and \c GrabPassengerAnimAndDuration (special-ability table)
///   go through their own custom parser, whose time handling is not
///   described here.
///
/// \see ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, ModuleFactory::newModule,
///      INI::initFromINIMultiProc, \ref sub_objects_modules,
///      \ref sub_rts_model_flow


/// \class SpecialPowerModule
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Base class of the special power modules, also registered on its
///        own as "SpecialPowerModule": it ties an object to one
///        \c SpecialPower definition and keeps that power's recharge and
///        pause state for the object.
///
/// The special power modules here (CashHackSpecialPower,
/// CombineHordeSpecialPower, HordeDispatchSpecialPower and
/// WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule) derive from it, as do the special power
/// modules on \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories. The
/// overrides read for this group (CashHackSpecialPower,
/// HordeDispatchSpecialPower and WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule) call the
/// base class's entry point (\c doSpecialPower, \c doSpecialPowerAtObject
/// or \c doSpecialPowerAtLocation) as part of their own. Used under its own
/// name, the module gives an object a power with the base behavior only;
/// its data factory is SpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData, so
/// such a block accepts exactly the special-power table.
///
/// Ownership and lifetime: one module per object whose template lists it,
/// created by SpecialPowerModule::friend_newModuleInstance and owned by
/// the object; it reads the template's shared SpecialPowerModuleData.
///
/// Its constructor (SpecialPowerModule::SpecialPowerModule) sets up the
/// timer: unless the object is still under construction or the power is
/// shared and synchronised across a player's objects, it starts the
/// recharge; with \c StartsPaused it pauses the countdown; and, when the
/// countdown is not paused, for a shared power with a public timer on a
/// structure with a controlling player it registers the power with the
/// in-game UI's superweapon display. Zero Hour's constructor has these
/// steps plus a \c resolveSpecialPower call, which the BFME 2 constructor
/// does not make. Unlike Zero Hour's, the BFME 2 constructor also makes
/// the power ready on the creation frame when \c AvailableAtStart is set
/// (BFME 1's special-power table already has that field).
///
/// \par Modding
/// Both switches the constructor reads are fields of the special-power
/// table: \c StartsPaused holds the countdown until something unpauses it
/// (for example UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade), and \c AvailableAtStart makes
/// the power ready on the frame the module is created.
///
/// \see SpecialPowerModuleData, SpecialPowerModule::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *SpecialPowerModule::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "SpecialPowerModule": creates
///        the plain base module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists \c SpecialPowerModule is built. It
/// allocates the module, runs SpecialPowerModule's constructor and returns
/// it; the group page describes the shared shape. Subclasses register
/// their own instance factories; this one is reached only through the
/// \c SpecialPowerModule name.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's SpecialPowerModuleData for this module
///                    line, shared with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see SpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData


/// \class CashHackSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Special power module registered as "CashHackSpecialPower": takes
///        money from the target object's player and gives it to the
///        owner's player.
///
/// CashHackSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtObject does nothing when the
/// owner is disabled or there is no target. Otherwise it runs
/// SpecialPowerModule::doSpecialPowerAtObject, then, when both controlling
/// players have a money store, takes the smaller of the victim player's
/// current money and the amount the module selects; the owner's own
/// balance is not tested. Only when that amount is above zero does it
/// withdraw it from the victim's player, deposit it with the owner's
/// player and show floating text from the string table: \c GUI:AddCash in
/// green above the owner and \c GUI:LoseCash in red above the victim, each
/// formatted with the amount. A victim with no money therefore loses
/// nothing and no text appears. Zero Hour: the amount of the first
/// \c UpgradeMoneyAmount entry, in INI order, whose science the owner's
/// player has; otherwise \c MoneyAmount. The BFME 2 selection step is not
/// described here.
///
/// It adds no per-object state to SpecialPowerModule (it is allocated at
/// the same size). Zero Hour: the module class likewise adds no data
/// members, and its CashHackSpecialPowerModuleData has the same two INI
/// fields (\c UpgradeMoneyAmount and \c MoneyAmount).
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of CashHackSpecialPowerModuleData: the special-power table,
/// then \c UpgradeMoneyAmount (repeatable; Zero Hour: each line pairs a
/// science with an amount) and \c MoneyAmount (integer). The floating
/// texts are string table entries and can be changed there.
///
/// \see CashHackSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class CashHackSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Module data for CashHackSpecialPower: the special-power fields
///        plus the amounts to steal.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// CashHackSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template. It holds a list of entries filled by
/// \c UpgradeMoneyAmount lines and a default amount from
/// \c MoneyAmount.

/// \fn ModuleData *CashHackSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "CashHackSpecialPower": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming \c CashHackSpecialPower. It runs the
/// constructor, then, when \p ini is not null, parses with
/// CashHackSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse, which adds
/// SpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse's table and then the two
/// cash-hack fields.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see CashHackSpecialPower


/// \class CombineHordeSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Module data for CombineHordeSpecialPower: the special-power
///        fields plus \c ScanRange.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// CombineHordeSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template.
/// \see CombineHordeSpecialPower

/// \fn ModuleData *CombineHordeSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "CombineHordeSpecialPower": creates
///        the module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It runs the constructor, then, when \p ini is not null, parses with
/// CombineHordeSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse, which adds the
/// special-power table and then a one-field table holding \c ScanRange
/// (real).
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see CombineHordeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class HordeDispatchSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Special power module registered as "HordeDispatchSpecialPower":
///        passes the power on to the members of its horde.
///
/// All three entry points first check that the owner passes a template
/// test and has a contain module, and do nothing otherwise; the members
/// are the objects that contain module reports. Then:
/// - HordeDispatchSpecialPower::doSpecialPower and
///   HordeDispatchSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtLocation run the base
///   class's version, then call the same entry point, with the same
///   arguments, on each member's special power module for the same
///   \c SpecialPower definition.
/// - HordeDispatchSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtObject calls each member's
///   module at the target first, reads back an object id from each
///   member's module (most likely the object it last targeted), keeps the
///   object farthest from the owner (starting
///   from the original target), and finally runs the base class's version
///   at that object.
///
/// Members without a module for the same power are skipped. The class
/// adds no per-object state to SpecialPowerModule. Its data accepts only
/// the special-power table (HordeDispatchSpecialPowerModuleData).
///
/// \par Modding
/// For the dispatch to reach a member, the member's own template must list
/// a special power module naming the same \c SpecialPowerTemplate as the
/// horde's module.
///
/// \see HordeDispatchSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      HordeDispatchSpecialPowerModuleData

/// \fn Module *HordeDispatchSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "HordeDispatchSpecialPower":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. Same shape
/// as SpecialPowerModule::friend_newModuleInstance, and the same
/// allocation size.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's HordeDispatchSpecialPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see HordeDispatchSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData


/// \class WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Special power module registered as
///        "WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule": toggles weapon-set flags on its
///        object.
///
/// WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule::doSpecialPower walks the weapon-set
/// flags named by \c FlagsUsedForToggle. Each one that is set on the
/// object is cleared; each one that is clear is set, and a model-condition
/// set naming \c MOVING, \c ATTACKING and two \c FIRING_OR_PREATTACK
/// conditions is then handed to an object helper this page does not
/// identify. Then:
/// - if any flag was switched on, it passes \c ToggleOnAttributeModifier
///   and \c ToggleOnSleepFrames to Object::addAttributeModifierToPool
///   (when the name is not empty and the count is not zero); when the
///   count is above zero it disables the object until that many frames
///   from now; and it always sets a special model condition for that
///   count;
/// - otherwise it does the same with \c ToggleOffAttributeModifier and
///   \c ToggleOffSleepFrames (with the same two guards), without the
///   model condition.
///
/// In both cases it finishes with SpecialPowerModule::doSpecialPower. It
/// adds no per-object state to SpecialPowerModule.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModuleData: the special-power
/// table, then \c FlagsUsedForToggle (weapon-set flag names),
/// \c ToggleOnSleepFrames and \c ToggleOffSleepFrames (integers, logic
/// frames), \c ToggleOnAttributeModifier and
/// \c ToggleOffAttributeModifier (attribute modifier names, stored as
/// text). The weapon sets themselves come from the object's
/// \c WeaponSet blocks; the flags only select among them.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The two sleep-frame counts are frames as written, not milliseconds
/// converted at load, so their real duration changes with the logic rate.
///
/// \see WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Module data for the "WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule" module: the
///        special-power fields plus the toggle settings.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template. Its constructor clears the flag set and
/// the two sleep-frame counts and empties both attribute modifier names.
/// \see WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule

/// \fn Module *WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. Same shape
/// as SpecialPowerModule::friend_newModuleInstance, and the same
/// allocation size.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule":
///        creates the module data and parses the module's INI block into
///        it.
///
/// The registration pairs this factory with the name
/// \c WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule, not with a separate
/// "WeaponChangeSpecialPower" name. It runs the constructor, then, when
/// \p ini is not null, parses with
/// WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse, which adds the
/// special-power table and then the five toggle fields.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see WeaponChangeSpecialPowerModule::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class ActivateModuleSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Special-ability update module registered as
///        "ActivateModuleSpecialPower": when the ability fires, it wakes or
///        fires other modules of the same object.
///
/// Its data (ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData) holds up to eight
/// \c TriggerSpecialPower entries, each naming another module of the same
/// object and carrying a position mode. ActivateModuleSpecialPower::SetWakeState
/// goes through the entries and looks each module up on the object:
/// - ActivateModuleSpecialPower::triggerAbilityEffect runs
///   SpecialAbilityUpdate::triggerAbilityEffect and then wakes the
///   entries: a named update module is set to run on the next frame, and a
///   named module without an update interface but with a special-power
///   interface is fired at a location, either the owner's position or the
///   ability's target position as the entry's mode selects.
/// - ActivateModuleSpecialPower::onExit (after SpecialAbilityUpdate's) and
///   ActivateModuleSpecialPower::onObjectCreated put the named update
///   modules to sleep indefinitely, so they stay idle until the ability
///   fires again.
///
/// Entries whose name finds no module are skipped. An entry that names the
/// ActivateModuleSpecialPower module itself is reported through the debug
/// library as "ActivateModuleSpecialPower can not be used on it self!",
/// with the object's template name.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields: the special-ability table, then \c TriggerSpecialPower
/// (\ref grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more describes the entry
/// format). Typical use, as the code shows, is to keep some update module
/// of the object asleep until a power is used, or to chain one power into
/// another on the same object.
///
/// \see ActivateModuleSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData

/// \fn Module *ActivateModuleSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "ActivateModuleSpecialPower":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared shape.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData


/// \class TeleportToCasterSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Special-ability update module registered as
///        "TeleportToCasterSpecialPower": moves objects found around the
///        target position to positions around its own object.
///
/// TeleportToCasterSpecialPower::triggerAbilityEffect runs
/// SpecialAbilityUpdate::triggerAbilityEffect and then, in the usual case,
/// collects the objects within \c Radius of the ability's target position
/// that pass a chain of partition filters, among them one built from the
/// module's \c SpecialPower definition and the owner's player. It skips objects in either of two status states and objects
/// linked, through an object id they hold, to an object whose template has
/// a certain flag (this page does not identify what that id records),
/// then hands
/// the list to
/// TeleportToCasterSpecialPower::teleportList with the owner's position as
/// the centre. When the owner also has a StoreObjectsSpecialPower module
/// whose stored object list is not empty, it teleports those objects to
/// positions around the target position instead, then empties that
/// module's list. Either way it finishes by playing \c TriggerFX at the
/// target position.
///
/// TeleportToCasterSpecialPower::teleportList spreads the objects evenly
/// round a circle: object number \e i starts its search at angle
/// 2 pi i / n, and PartitionManager::findPositionAround looks for a free
/// spot between \c MinDestinationRadius and \c MaxDestinationRadius of the
/// centre; when none is found the object goes to the centre itself. Each
/// object's AI is set idle, it is turned to face away from the centre (or
/// keeps its facing when no spot was found) and placed at its new
/// position. \c TargetFX is applied to each object before and after the
/// move (played on the object, or handed to an object-side helper this
/// page does not identify when one is present).
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of TeleportToCasterSpecialPowerModuleData: the
/// special-ability table, then \c Radius, \c MinDestinationRadius and
/// \c MaxDestinationRadius (reals) and \c TriggerFX and \c TargetFX (FX
/// lists). Which objects are pulled depends partly on the \c SpecialPower
/// definition (through the filter above); the other filters and the
/// status and template-flag exclusions are hard-coded. The stored-object
/// path needs both modules on the same object: a StoreObjectsSpecialPower
/// module that has stored objects, and this one to send them to the
/// target.
///
/// \see TeleportToCasterSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      TeleportToCasterSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
///      StoreObjectsSpecialPower

