/// \class UnleashSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Special-ability update module registered as
///        "UnleashSpecialPower": acts on the object its owner's
///        SlaveWatcherBehavior module refers to.
///
/// Both overrides look up the owner's \c SlaveWatcherBehavior module and
/// the object whose id it holds, and stop if either is missing.
/// - UnleashSpecialPower::triggerAbilityEffect first runs
///   SpecialAbilityUpdate::triggerAbilityEffect, so the base effect
///   happens even when the lookups fail; it then goes through that
///   object's behavior modules; for each one that answers a particular
///   interface query it calls the returned interface and, when the object
///   has an AI, orders it to hunt (an AI hunt command issued from the AI
///   itself). What the interface is, and therefore what is released, is
///   not identified here.
/// - A second override, a yes-or-no check on the same object that also
///   fails when one of the object's own flags is set, is not named here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// UnleashSpecialPowerModuleData accepts only the special-ability table;
/// its own table is empty. The power does nothing on an object without a
/// \c SlaveWatcherBehavior module.
///
/// \see UnleashSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *UnleashSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "UnleashSpecialPower": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. Same shape
/// and allocation size as ActivateModuleSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's UnleashSpecialPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see UnleashSpecialPower


/// \class CurseSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Special-ability update module registered as "CurseSpecialPower":
///        applies a curse percentage to enemy objects at the target.
///
/// CurseSpecialPower::triggerAbilityEffect runs
/// SpecialAbilityUpdate::triggerAbilityEffect. When the ability has a
/// target object id, it curses that object if it still exists and does
/// nothing otherwise; without a target id, it curses every object that
/// partition filters select within the \c SpecialPower definition's radius
/// of the target position. It then plays \c TriggerFX at the target
/// position, in the single-object case only when the object was found and
/// in the area case only when at least one object was found (whether or
/// not any of them passed the curse tests below).
///
/// Cursing one object skips the owner itself, objects whose template lacks
/// a certain flag, objects that are not enemies of the owner, and objects
/// in either of two status states. Otherwise it passes \c CursePercentage
/// to an Object routine (not named here) and plays \c CursedFX on the
/// object. What the percentage changes on the object is not established
/// here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of CurseSpecialPowerModuleData: the special-ability table,
/// then \c TriggerFX and \c CursedFX (FX lists) and \c CursePercentage
/// (a percentage, stored as a fraction). The area radius is the
/// \c SpecialPower definition's, not a module field. The enemy test and the
/// status and template exclusions are hard-coded.
///
/// \see CurseSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      CurseSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class CurseSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Module data for CurseSpecialPower: the special-ability fields
///        plus two FX lists and the curse percentage.
///
/// Built once per module line by
