/// \class UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade": while
///        its upgrade is active, the object's matching special power is
///        allowed to count down.
///
/// UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade::upgradeImplementation goes through the
/// object's behavior modules and, for each special power module whose
/// \c SpecialPower definition is the one named by \c SpecialPowerTemplate,
/// unpauses its countdown; unless \c ObeyRechageOnTrigger is set, it also
/// makes the power ready from the current frame.
/// UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade::upgradeRemovalImplementation pauses the same
/// modules again (after a guard check this page does not describe), and
/// UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade::onCreate registers the module with its
/// object through an Object routine this page does not name.
///
/// The natural partner is a special power module with \c StartsPaused set
/// (SpecialPowerModule): the power stays paused until the upgrade arrives.
///
/// Zero Hour: the same class only unpauses the matching modules, and its
/// data has only \c SpecialPowerTemplate. The ready-at-once step (with
/// its flag) and the removal step are not in Zero Hour; Open-BFME-1's
/// UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade already has both.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgradeModuleData: the shared upgrade
/// table, then \c SpecialPowerTemplate (a \c SpecialPower name, looked up
/// when the INI is read; INI::parseSpecialPowerTemplate stores null for an
/// unknown name without reporting it) and \c ObeyRechageOnTrigger
/// (boolean; the misspelling is the retail field name). With the flag clear, gaining
/// the upgrade makes the power usable at once; with it set, the power
/// keeps its remaining recharge time.
///
/// \see UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgradeModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Module data for UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade: the shared upgrade
///        fields plus the special power to unpause and the recharge flag.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template.
/// \see UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade

/// \fn ModuleData *UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade":
///        creates the module data and parses the module's INI block into
///        it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It runs the constructor, then, when \p ini is not null, parses with
/// UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgradeModuleData::buildFieldParse, which adds the
/// shared upgrade table and then the class's two fields.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade


/// \class AudioLoopUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "AudioLoopUpgrade": plays a sound
///        on its object while its upgrade is active, optionally for a
///        limited time; a \c KillOnDeath switch stops it in a further
///        step (most likely on death).
///
/// It is an update module as well as an upgrade module, and its
/// registration adds the die interface.
/// - AudioLoopUpgrade::upgradeImplementation stops the sound it started
///   earlier, if any, starts the \c SoundToPlay audio event attached to its
///   object through the audio manager and keeps the returned handle. When
///   \c KillAfterMS is above zero, it sets the module to wake that many
///   logic frames later (the value is converted to frames when the INI is
///   read).
/// - AudioLoopUpgrade::update, run on that wake-up, stops the sound and
///   puts the module to sleep indefinitely.
/// - AudioLoopUpgrade::upgradeRemovalImplementation stops the sound and
///   puts the module to sleep indefinitely.
/// - A further override stops the sound in the same way when
///   \c KillOnDeath is set; given that test and the die interface, it is
///   most likely the death handler, but it is not named here.
/// Each of these four steps skips the audio calls when there is no audio
/// manager.
///
/// AudioLoopUpgrade::xfer saves a version, the sound handle (through the
/// audio manager's handle transfer), the update module state and the
/// upgrade state. Unlike the steps above, it calls the audio manager
/// without testing that one exists.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of AudioLoopUpgradeModuleData: the shared upgrade table, a
/// death-condition table (\c DeathTypes, \c ExemptStatus,
/// \c RequiredStatus, \c DamageAmountRequired, \c MinKillerAngle and
/// \c MaxKillerAngle), then \c SoundToPlay (an audio event),
/// \c KillAfterMS (milliseconds) and \c KillOnDeath (boolean). Zero
/// Hour's die modules share a similar table: \c DeathTypes,
/// \c VeterancyLevels, \c ExemptStatus and \c RequiredStatus. The BFME 2
/// table has no \c VeterancyLevels and adds \c DamageAmountRequired and
/// the two killer angles. Whether the
/// death-condition fields gate the KillOnDeath step is not established
/// here. The sound itself, including whether it loops, is defined by the
/// audio event.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The sound handle is part of the saved state and goes through the audio
/// manager's own transfer step, so a replacement audio layer has to supply
/// that step for saves to load.
///
/// \see AudioLoopUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      AudioLoopUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class AudioLoopUpgradeModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Module data for AudioLoopUpgrade: the shared upgrade fields, the
///        death-condition fields and the sound settings.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// AudioLoopUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.
/// \see AudioLoopUpgrade

/// \fn Module *AudioLoopUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AudioLoopUpgrade": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared shape.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's AudioLoopUpgradeModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AudioLoopUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *AudioLoopUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "AudioLoopUpgrade": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It runs the constructor, then, when \p ini is not null, parses with
/// AudioLoopUpgradeModuleData::buildFieldParse, which adds three tables:
/// the shared upgrade table, the death-condition table and the class's
/// own three fields.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see AudioLoopUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class BaseUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "BaseUpgrade": when its upgrade
///        fires, it builds a structure for the owner's player at a bone of
///        the owner's model.
///
/// BaseUpgrade::upgradeImplementation needs an owner with a controlling
/// player and a drawable, and a template named \c BuildingTemplateName
/// (looked up with ThingFactory::findTemplate); it does nothing if any is
/// missing. It asks the drawable for the pristine bone positions named by
/// \c PlacementPrefix (at most 32) and, when \c PlacementIndex is above
/// zero and below the number of bones found, converts that bone to a world
/// position and facing. Otherwise the new structure takes the owner's own
/// position and facing. It then creates the object with
/// ThingFactory::newObject on a team of the controlling player, records
/// the owner as its producer and builder, places and turns it, adds it to
/// the pathfinding map and reports it to the player with
/// Player::onStructureCreated.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of BaseUpgradeModuleData: the shared upgrade table, then
/// \c BuildingTemplateName and \c PlacementPrefix (text) and
/// \c PlacementIndex (integer). The template name is looked up only when
/// the upgrade fires; an unknown name builds nothing, without a message
/// from this module. An index of zero or less, or one not below the number
/// of bones found, falls back to the owner's position, and at most 32
/// bones with the prefix are considered.
///
/// \see BaseUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      BaseUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class BaseUpgradeModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Module data for BaseUpgrade: the shared upgrade fields plus the
///        structure to build and where to put it.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// BaseUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every object
/// of the template.
/// \see BaseUpgrade

/// \fn Module *BaseUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "BaseUpgrade": creates the module
///        for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared shape; the allocation size is the same as
/// ArmorUpgrade's, ModelConditionUpgrade's and UnpauseSpecialPowerUpgrade's.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's BaseUpgradeModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see BaseUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *BaseUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "BaseUpgrade": creates the module
///        data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It runs the constructor, then, when \p ini is not null, parses with
/// BaseUpgradeModuleData::buildFieldParse, which adds the shared upgrade
/// table and then the class's three fields.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see BaseUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class ModelConditionUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "ModelConditionUpgrade": by its
///        fields, it changes its object's model condition flags when its
///        upgrade fires.
///
/// Its BFME 2 bodies are not described here; the class
/// page records the INI contract from the retail field table. Zero Hour:
/// the class sets one model condition flag (\c ConditionFlag) on its
/// object when the upgrade fires. The BFME 2 field table has five fields
/// in place of \c ConditionFlag, so its INI contract is broader. By their
/// names they add flags, remove flags and set a temporary flag with a
/// time; what the code does with them is not described here.
/// \c RemoveConditionFlags and \c RemoveConditionFlagsInRange write the
/// same stored set, with different parsers.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of ModelConditionUpgradeModuleData: the shared upgrade table,
/// then \c AddConditionFlags, \c RemoveConditionFlags,
/// \c RemoveConditionFlagsInRange, \c AddTempConditionFlag and
/// \c TempConditionTime (real). \c AddTempConditionFlag uses the same
/// parser as the special-power table's \c SetModelCondition. Model
/// condition names must be ones the executable knows; the art that reacts
/// to them is defined in the object's draw module.
///
/// \see ModelConditionUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class ModelConditionUpgradeModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Module data for ModelConditionUpgrade: the shared upgrade fields
///        plus the condition flags to add and remove.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// ModelConditionUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template.
/// \see ModelConditionUpgrade

/// \fn ModuleData *ModelConditionUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "ModelConditionUpgrade": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It runs the constructor, then, when \p ini is not null, parses with
/// ModelConditionUpgradeModuleData::buildFieldParse, which adds the shared
/// upgrade table and then the class's five fields.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see ModelConditionUpgrade


/// \class ArmorUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "ArmorUpgrade": switches an armor
///        set flag on its object's body, and the model condition that goes
///        with it, when its upgrade is applied or removed.
///
/// ArmorUpgrade::upgradeImplementation does nothing when
/// \c IgnoreArmorUpgrade is set. Otherwise it runs a shared upgrade helper
/// and, when the object has a body module, sets the armor set flag named by
/// \c ArmorSetFlag on the body (BodyModuleInterface::setArmorSetFlag) and
/// sets the model condition paired with that flag. With
/// \c KillArmorUpgrade set it does the opposite: it clears the flag and
/// the condition. ArmorUpgrade::upgradeRemovalImplementation reverses
/// whichever of the two the upgrade did and then runs a second shared
/// helper; it too does nothing with \c IgnoreArmorUpgrade. ArmorUpgrade::xfer
/// saves only a version and the shared upgrade module state.
///
/// Zero Hour: the class sets one fixed armor set flag (the player upgrade
/// set), has no fields of its own, and has a hard-coded case that adds a
/// terrain decal when triggered by the upgrade named
/// \c Upgrade_AmericaChemicalSuits; the BFME 2 bodies have no such case.
/// Choosing the flag, the kill and ignore switches and the paired model
/// condition are not in Zero Hour; BFME 1's ArmorUpgrade already has them.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of ArmorUpgradeModuleData: the shared upgrade table, then
/// \c KillArmorUpgrade and \c IgnoreArmorUpgrade (booleans) and
/// \c ArmorSetFlag (one armor set flag name). The armor values for each
/// set come from the object's \c ArmorSet blocks; this module only
/// selects which set conditions are active. The model condition paired
/// with each armor set flag comes from a table in the executable's data,
/// not from an INI field.
///
/// \see ArmorUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData, ArmorUpgrade::xfer

/// \class ArmorUpgradeModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Module data for ArmorUpgrade: the shared upgrade fields plus the
///        armor set flag and the two switches.
///
/// Built once per module line by ArmorUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData
/// and shared by every object of the template.
///
/// \par Implementation note
/// ArmorUpgradeModuleData::buildFieldParse keeps its own field table in
/// writable data and completes it the first time it runs: only then does
/// the \c ArmorSetFlag entry receive the list of accepted names and the
/// place it stores into.
/// \see ArmorUpgrade

/// \fn ModuleData *ArmorUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "ArmorUpgrade": creates the module
///        data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It allocates the data, runs the constructor and, when \p ini is not
/// null, parses with ArmorUpgradeModuleData::buildFieldParse (the shared
/// upgrade table, then the class's three fields). It is the one data
/// factory in this group without an exception frame: if the constructor
/// threw, nothing would free the block. The constructor is short and has
/// no exception frame either: it runs a base constructor whose identity is
/// not established, installs a virtual table that retail shares with other
/// module data classes (identical tables were folded together), and stores
/// the defaults (both switches off and a fixed default armor set flag).
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see ArmorUpgrade


/// \class CostModifierUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "CostModifierUpgrade": by its
///        fields and its Zero Hour namesake, a percentage change to what
///        the controlling player pays.
///
/// Its BFME 2 bodies are not described here; the class page records the
/// INI contract from the retail field table. Zero Hour: the class adds a production cost change for the objects of
/// the given kinds to the controlling player when the upgrade fires,
/// removes it again when the object is deleted (\c onDelete, and only if
/// the upgrade had run), and moves it to the new owner when the object is
/// captured (\c onCapture). Zero Hour selects the objects with
/// \c EffectKindOf; the BFME 2 field table has \c ObjectFilter in its
/// place, plus fields that, by name, extend the change to upgrade costs and
/// to the palantir display. These are not new in BFME 2: BFME 1's retail
/// field table for this module data already lists \c ObjectFilter,
/// \c UpgradeDiscount, \c StartsActive and \c LabelForPalantirString. Of
/// the BFME 2 fields, only \c ApplyToTheseUpgrades and \c Slaughter are
/// absent from that table.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of CostModifierUpgradeModuleData: the shared upgrade table,
/// then \c ObjectFilter (an object filter), \c Percentage (a percentage),
/// \c UpgradeDiscount, \c StartsActive and \c Slaughter (booleans),
/// \c ApplyToTheseUpgrades (upgrade names) and \c LabelForPalantirString
/// (text). \c ApplyToTheseUpgrades uses INI::parseAsciiStringVector, which
/// empties the list before reading a line, so only the last such line in a
/// block counts; write every name on one line.
///
/// \see CostModifierUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class CostModifierUpgradeModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Module data for CostModifierUpgrade: the shared upgrade fields
///        plus the filter, the percentage and the options.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// CostModifierUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.
/// \see CostModifierUpgrade

/// \fn ModuleData *CostModifierUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "CostModifierUpgrade": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It runs the constructor, then, when \p ini is not null, parses with
/// CostModifierUpgradeModuleData::buildFieldParse, which adds the shared
/// upgrade table and then the class's seven fields.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see CostModifierUpgrade


/// \class CommandPointsUpgradeModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_special_power_upgrade_factories_rest
/// \brief Module data for CommandPointsUpgrade: the shared upgrade fields
